  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 1231 PM CDT Sat Oct 10 2026\n\nSeveral record high temperatures were broken yesterday across the \narea and several more are threatened again this afternoon. The large \nand amplified ridge remains situated over the Great Plains into \nsouthern Canada. To the east, the remnants of Isaias, and to the \nwest, a digging trough, combine to tighten the pressure gradient \nacross the region. Strong southerly winds have developed as a result \nwith peak gusts between 30-40+ mph later this afternoon. These winds \nand clear skies promote excellent mixing of warmer and drier air \naloft as well. Another afternoon of Summer-like temperatures is \nanticipated with upper 80s and even low 90s for many. These \ntemperatures may break several records across the area. Omaha's \nrecord high is 91 set in 1928 with a current forecast high of 92. \nSimilarly, Lincoln has a forecast high of 92 which would break the \nprevious record of 91 set in 2024. Norfolk may fall shy of their \nrecord of 94 set in 1928 with a high of 91 forecast. \n\nThe dry and windy conditions are leading to increased fire weather \nconcerns. Minimum relative humidity between 25-35 percent and wind \ngusts of 30-40+ mph may support rapid fire spread. The windiest and \ndriest conditions overlap over portions of northeast Nebraska where \nvery high fire danger is possible.\n\nTemperatures remain mild heading into Sunday with continued strong \nsouth winds. Temperatures in the 60s start the day before another \nwarm afternoon. The latest forecast guidance has slowed down the \nprogression of large-scale features over the CONUS and this keeps \nthe heat in place. High temperatures should once again climb well \ninto the 80s and even low 90s despite increased cloud cover from a \npassing disturbance to our north. Temperatures won't be quite as \nwarm as today and won't break records, but remain well above normal \nregardless. Unfortunately, the slower progression of features has \nslowed Monday's cold front. Another warm day is anticipated, but \nwith the added compressional warming ahead of the front, even warmer \ntemperatures than Sunday appear likely.\n\nThe aforementioned cold front finally pushes south through the area \non Tuesday. This begins a cooling trend for the region. At the same \ntime, our next weather system approaches the Great Plains. A trough \nover the Four Corners will eject northeast and absorb remnant \nmoisture from Simon in the process. Rain chances return to the \nforecast late Tuesday and these chances continue into Thursday \nbefore another cold front pushes through the region as the system \nmoves off to the east. Expected rainfall totals from this system \nhave decreased slightly since yesterday, but widespread 0.25 to 0.75 \ninch rainfall totals appear likely based on ensemble guidance. The \nhighest totals will be across southeast Nebraska with lower amounts \ntowards northeast Nebraska. \n\nBeyond the mid-week system, things turn quiet and cooler. A strong \nsurface high pressure settles into the Great Plains late next week \ninto next weekend. Fall-like temperatures return to the region with \nplentiful sunshine. A welcomed return to more seasonal weather.",
